    <h1 id="about">MathEconLib</h1>
    <p class="tagline">Lean 4 libraries of definitions and theorems for new research in economics.</p>
    <p class="draft"><strong>Collaborator draft.</strong> Items in <span class="placeholder">amber italics</span> need confirming.</p>
    <p class="lede">MathEconLib is a long-term project to build software libraries of mathematical definitions, theorems, and machine-checked proofs for economics. Its first library, <code>auctionlib</code>, formalizes auctions and pricing; the intended scope spans microeconomic theory, macroeconomic theory, and econometrics.</p>
    <h2 id="research">Use existing theorems to prove new ones</h2>
    <p>Researchers can import definitions, state a model's assumptions, and apply existing lemmas in a new proof. In <code>auctionlib</code>, the envelope theorem is proved for a general single-parameter problem and then applied to bidder problems. A researcher can use that theorem in a new model without proving it again.</p>
    <p>Lean checks each proof against the definitions, assumptions, and earlier theorems it uses. If a new model changes a definition or assumption, that change is visible in its definitions or theorem statements, making its relationship to earlier formalized results precise.</p>
    <p>MathEconLib reuses <a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4">Mathlib</a>’s mathematical foundations, contributes broadly reusable results upstream, and maintains economics-specific definitions and theorems in its own libraries. The project is developed with <a href="https://axiommath.ai">Axiom Math</a>.</p>
    <p>MathEconLib is not an end-to-end autoformalization project. Its definitions and conventions are developed by economic theorists, with the aim of building a trusted foundation for formal research in economics. AI-assisted tools help with formalization and proof development, but researchers determine the mathematical architecture and Lean's kernel checks every theorem.</p>

    <h2 id="vision">Long-term scope</h2>
    <p>MathEconLib is intended to grow as a family of interoperable libraries covering the major mathematical areas of economics.</p>
    <dl class="scope">
      <dt>Microeconomic theory</dt>
      <dd>Preferences and choice, games, information, contracts, market and mechanism design, industrial organization, and general equilibrium.</dd>
      <dt>Macroeconomic theory</dt>
      <dd>Dynamic optimization, recursive methods, growth, heterogeneous-agent models, and monetary and fiscal theory.</dd>
      <dt>Econometrics</dt>
      <dd>Probability and statistical foundations, identification, estimation, inference, and causal and structural methods.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="auctionlib">First library: auctionlib</h2>
    <p>MathEconLib begins with <code>auctionlib</code>, its first substantial library. It formalizes the single-parameter theory of auctions and pricing, from incentive compatibility through revenue equivalence, together with the game-theoretic foundations those results assume. Its source is on <a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib">GitHub</a>.</p>
    <p>The library keeps apart three things that textbooks usually blend:</p>

    <ol class="stack" aria-label="Architecture layers">
      <li class="layer"><code>Mechanism n</code><span>the rules of the game: an allocation rule <em>x</em> and a payment rule <em>t</em> over the vector of reports</span></li>
      <li class="step">add random types for each bidder on a probability space (Ω, ℙ)</li>
      <li class="layer"><code>Auction Ω n</code><span>a mechanism inside its probabilistic environment</span></li>
      <li class="step">fix one bidder and the strategy the others play</li>
      <li class="layer"><code>BidderProblem</code><span>that bidder's interim decision problem: win probability, cost, strategy</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p>A <code>BidderProblem</code> is a special case of a <code>SingleParameterProblem</code>, which covers any quasilinear problem with payoff <em>u</em>(<em>v</em>,<em>a</em>) = <em>v</em>·<em>x</em>(<em>a</em>) − <em>t</em>(<em>a</em>), whether the action is a bid, a report, a contract choice, or a purchase decision. The envelope theorem and the payment identity are proved once at that level and reused by every application.</p>

    <h3>Current results in auctionlib</h3>
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Envelope theorem: <em>U</em> is Lipschitz, <em>U′</em> = <em>q</em> almost everywhere, integral representation</td><td><a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/blob/main/Auctionlib/BidderProblem/EnvelopeIC.lean"><code>BidderProblem/EnvelopeIC</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Myerson's lemma, both directions</td><td><a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/blob/main/Auctionlib/BidderProblem/TypeDeviationIC.lean"><code>BidderProblem/TypeDeviationIC</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Revenue equivalence; expected revenue equals E[<em>S</em><sub>2</sub>] under symmetric independent private values</td><td><a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/blob/main/Auctionlib/Auction/RevenueEquivalence.lean"><code>Auction/RevenueEquivalence</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Seller profit as expected virtual surplus; posted-price optimality conditions</td><td><a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/tree/main/Auctionlib/ValueDistribution"><code>ValueDistribution/</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>All-purchase auctions: explicit equilibria, revenue and welfare decompositions, optimal pricing</td><td><a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/tree/main/Auctionlib/Auction/AllPurchase"><code>Auction/AllPurchase/</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Winner's-curse inequality for affiliated signals on finite lattices</td><td><a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/blob/main/Auctionlib/Auction/Affiliation/WinnersCurse.lean"><code>Auction/Affiliation/WinnersCurse</code></a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Existence of pure and mixed Nash equilibria, via Kakutani–Glicksberg–Fan</td><td><a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/blob/main/Auctionlib/GameTheory/NashExistence.lean"><code>GameTheory/NashExistence</code></a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The <a href="https://github.com/AxiomMath/auctionlib/blob/main/README.md">README</a> indexes every file and theorem and is the authoritative list; this page shows only the headline results.</p>
    <p>Every result compiles without <code>sorry</code>, and <code>#print axioms</code> on the main theorems shows only <code>propext</code>, <code>Classical.choice</code>, and <code>Quot.sound</code>. The statements of the core theorems are pinned in a separate file that the build checks, so a change to any public statement breaks the build loudly rather than silently.</p>

